{"body":"This resolves the unnumbered alternating antimorphic conjecture after Theorem 23 on printed page 2220 of Nowotka's contribution, joint with Bischoff, to OWR 37/2010. For any antimorphic involution and positive alternating periods p,q of a word w, length at least p+q−gcd(p,q) forces alternating period gcd(p,q), including equality and a partial final block.\n\nThe proof extends w to the common finite word theta(w)w, applies classical Fine–Wilf to its ordinary periods 2p and 2q, and recovers the alternating phase from the central reflection. The three-page research note credits Fine–Wilf and Bischoff's earlier reflection and doubled-period mechanism. The reversal example abb shows that the threshold cannot be lowered uniformly by one; optimality for every numerical pair is not claimed. The separate morphic Conjecture 27 is outside this result.\n\nValidation is complete for the universal proof and original problem scope: three independent mathematical approach families, a bounded primary-literature priority audit, and two sequential NEW adversarial reviews of the final preprint and package. Both fresh reviewers independently analyzed the source and proof before seeing previous audits, reproduced the entire package outputs, and designed independent falsifiers; root independently reproduced their controls and verified every closed evidence record. No mandatory finding remains. The priority audit found no earlier exact theorem in inspected sources; its recorded access and version gaps remain, and global novelty or first discovery is not certified.\n\nThe final note, PDF, deposit metadata and public verification ZIP are in `problems/30001552_antimorphic_periods/preprint/`; `PREPRINT_RELEASE.md` records the accepted scope and hashes. The ZIP has 69 members, checks 68 payloads, and supports complete public integrity and finite-control reproduction without network access. Current author/portable runs reproduce 526887 and 68408 assertions respectively. The historical optional 68413 mode includes five private source hashes and remains unreproduced; no unavailable historical source check is represented as passed.\n\nAll 16 originally submitted problem files remain byte-identical. The shared queue update changes only this problem's status, turn count and verification note; all other queue bytes are preserved. Submitted and accepted status is `claimed_solved`, author turns `1/5`. Exact live Git/API source and final package verification are required immediately before acceptance, followed by a fresh actual-merge verification before publication.\n\nAI tools were used extensively in solving, mathematical verification, literature research, writing and adversarial review. This is an unrefereed preprint without independent external human peer review. Author: Alec Kriebel, Independent researcher, ORCID 0009-0001-9320-500X. Published version1.0: [10.5281/zenodo.23130727](https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.23130727) ([Zenodo record](https://zenodo.org/records/23130727)). Both whole public downloads and all10 supplied metadata fields match exactly, with zero normalization; DOI resolves HTTP200. One tracker row was appended and independently read back at 'Math Puzzles'!A17:D17. Full acceptance/publication workflow100%. Actual merge: 2c02f168cb6d764b33a9ea2af86506fbe5af44d8.\n","headRefOid":"4998409f32fd0b9b0cb96360c7666832ad6cfc9a","mergeCommit":{"oid":"2c02f168cb6d764b33a9ea2af86506fbe5af44d8"},"state":"MERGED"}
